WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.860
So if you look at the code for, you know, some of the Internet's oldest, like most critical infrastructure, you actually won't find a perfectly preserved machine.

00:09.860 --> 00:11.660
No, not at all. You really don't.

00:11.740 --> 00:19.640
Right. You'll find like a graveyard of abandoned ideas and patched over mistakes and totally degraded syntax.

00:19.900 --> 00:21.440
Yeah, it's honestly a mess under the hood.

00:21.560 --> 00:28.200
But the crazy thing is somehow it still routes your emails today, which brings up this massive question.

00:28.760 --> 00:34.820
Why do some systems survive total degradation while others just, you know, collapse overnight?

00:34.820 --> 00:39.620
And that paradox is, well, it's the exact entry point into what we're looking at today.

00:39.720 --> 00:39.880
Yeah.

00:40.020 --> 00:45.020
Because it fundamentally challenges our core assumption that survival means remaining intact.

00:45.300 --> 00:47.080
Right. Like staying exactly the same.

00:47.220 --> 00:50.820
Exactly. I mean, we tend to view structural health like a medical diagnosis.

00:51.300 --> 00:56.240
You know, you look at an X-ray of a broken arm, you see a jagged white line and it's a binary state.

00:56.400 --> 00:58.120
It's either broken or it's fine.

00:58.200 --> 01:00.040
Right. It either functions or it doesn't.

01:00.040 --> 01:04.280
But when you examine complex structures, that binary just completely falls apart.

01:04.660 --> 01:08.400
It totally does. And welcome to the Steep Dive, everyone.

01:08.940 --> 01:14.460
Our mission today is to explore a really profound, overarching question.

01:14.660 --> 01:18.600
Probably the most critical question we can ask about the systems we rely on, honestly.

01:18.800 --> 01:24.420
Yeah, exactly. The question is, how do things survive when the conditions that created them disappear?

01:24.420 --> 01:26.580
It's basically the ultimate survival problem.

01:26.700 --> 01:26.880
Yeah.

01:26.880 --> 01:37.580
And we aren't just talking about the survival of, like, biological organisms here. It's the survival of abstract structures, human institutions, and even cognitive concepts.

01:37.680 --> 01:43.500
It's huge. And to tackle this, we have a massive, incredibly dense stack of source material in front of us.

01:43.500 --> 01:44.760
A hundred pages, actually.

01:44.840 --> 01:53.240
Right. A hundred page metadocument. It's a catalog of the Flyxion-collected works. And for those of you who are unfamiliar, Flyxion isn't just, like, a single study.

01:53.340 --> 01:54.860
No, it's practically a whole universe.

01:54.860 --> 02:02.600
Yeah, it's an intricate universe of monographs, software, cosmological theories, and even experimental fiction.

02:02.780 --> 02:04.040
Which is wild to think about.

02:04.260 --> 02:17.820
It really is. And all of these disparate pieces are basically trying to solve this one survival problem of structures, be they physical buildings, digital code bases, languages, or, you know, human cognition itself.

02:17.920 --> 02:18.100
Right.

02:18.100 --> 02:25.120
Okay, let's unpack this. Because, I mean, where do we even begin with a document of this magnitude?

02:25.480 --> 02:31.480
Well, what's fascinating here is that this entire corpus didn't actually start out asking a question about survival.

02:31.780 --> 02:32.640
Oh, right.

02:32.920 --> 02:40.760
Yeah, it didn't begin as this focused investigation into why authors die, or why languages drift, or, you know, why software inevitably breaks.

02:41.000 --> 02:42.220
So how did it start then?

02:42.420 --> 02:44.920
The researchers actually just kind of stumbled into the survival question.

02:44.960 --> 02:45.780
I love that.

02:45.780 --> 02:50.140
Yeah, they hit it through a series of, like, spectacular scientific and philosophical failures.

02:50.340 --> 02:54.360
I love a good scientific failure. I mean, it's almost always where the real story starts, right?

02:54.380 --> 02:54.940
Oh, absolutely.

02:55.300 --> 03:08.060
So before we can understand how things actually survive, the source text tells us we have to look at why our standard, you know, traditional ways of measuring systems utterly fail to explain persistence.

03:08.060 --> 03:15.940
Right. So the early research detailed in the affliction corpus focused on the metrics we usually prioritize in science and systems engineering.

03:16.060 --> 03:16.980
Standard stuff.

03:17.220 --> 03:20.880
Exactly. Information, representation, prediction, and optimization.

03:21.220 --> 03:21.500
Okay.

03:21.840 --> 03:24.300
And the baseline assumption was pretty straightforward.

03:24.940 --> 03:30.980
If you can model those four pillars, you understand the system, and you can guarantee its survival.

03:31.220 --> 03:32.260
But they hit a wall.

03:32.340 --> 03:33.500
An absolute brick wall.

03:33.500 --> 03:39.320
Let's break that wall down piece by piece, because this is where the text gets incredibly critical of our modern paradigms.

03:39.400 --> 03:40.220
It pulls no punches.

03:40.580 --> 03:41.460
It really doesn't.

03:41.640 --> 03:43.140
Let's take representation first.

03:43.260 --> 03:51.000
I mean, the classic idea is that if a system has a complete high-fidelity representation of itself or its environment, it's safe.

03:51.360 --> 03:51.760
Right.

03:51.760 --> 03:56.700
But representation doesn't actually guarantee a system can be rebuilt if it gets damaged.

03:57.220 --> 03:57.500
Why not?

03:57.720 --> 04:00.360
The text makes this crucial philosophical distinction.

04:01.500 --> 04:04.020
The representation is not the thing itself.

04:04.220 --> 04:05.400
Ah, okay.

04:05.580 --> 04:11.080
Think about having an accurate, millimeter-perfect map of a city at one exact moment.

04:11.540 --> 04:15.620
That doesn't give you the capacity to reconstruct the city after an earthquake.

04:15.920 --> 04:17.640
Because a map doesn't pour concrete.

04:17.760 --> 04:19.840
Exactly. A map doesn't pour concrete.

04:19.840 --> 04:22.480
I'm picturing, like, an architect's blueprint here.

04:22.700 --> 04:23.960
That's a great way to look at it.

04:24.280 --> 04:28.280
If a hurricane destroys a house, having the blueprint is obviously helpful.

04:28.860 --> 04:36.460
But if the specific timber and steel required by that blueprint no longer exists in the local supply chain, well, the blueprint is useless.

04:36.840 --> 04:37.800
Yes, precisely.

04:38.000 --> 04:41.840
The representation survived, but the capacity to rebuild didn't.

04:42.680 --> 04:43.860
That's the core problem.

04:43.860 --> 04:50.320
A representation inherently assumes a static environment where the building blocks remain constant.

04:50.480 --> 04:51.140
Of course, they never do.

04:51.460 --> 04:51.660
Right.

04:52.500 --> 04:58.420
When the conditions that created the structure disappear, the representation just becomes this historical artifact.

04:58.940 --> 05:00.900
It's not a survival mechanism anymore.

05:01.020 --> 05:02.360
Okay, so that's representation down.

05:02.420 --> 05:03.800
So they moved on to prediction, right?

05:03.840 --> 05:04.360
They did, yeah.

05:04.360 --> 05:13.660
And the thought process there being, well, if a system is perfectly optimized to predict the future, surely it can avoid catastrophe and survive.

05:13.800 --> 05:16.020
But prediction models are incredibly fragile.

05:16.080 --> 05:16.360
How sick?

05:16.360 --> 05:20.820
Because they have no mechanism for reconstructing a broken past.

05:21.660 --> 05:24.440
Prediction and repair are fundamentally different.

05:24.680 --> 05:26.260
Almost opposed operations.

05:26.420 --> 05:27.160
Oh, that makes sense.

05:27.160 --> 05:32.840
Yeah, a predictive model just takes the current state of a system and projects it forward based on historical trends.

05:33.000 --> 05:34.760
Right, it assumes the line keeps going.

05:34.960 --> 05:35.260
Exactly.

05:35.680 --> 05:38.540
But what happens if the current state is corrupted?

05:39.060 --> 05:41.260
The model doesn't know how to fix it.

05:41.740 --> 05:44.880
It just confidently predicts a corrupted future.

05:45.220 --> 05:45.680
Oh, wow.

05:45.940 --> 05:48.980
I mean, we see this all the time with financial algorithms, don't we?

05:49.160 --> 05:49.740
All the time.

05:49.800 --> 05:55.740
Like an algorithmic trading model predicts market movements perfectly based on, say, the last 10 years of data.

05:55.940 --> 05:56.200
Right.

05:56.200 --> 06:02.060
But then a black swan event happens, a global pandemic, or a sudden geopolitical crisis.

06:02.300 --> 06:03.960
And the model completely breaks down.

06:04.160 --> 06:11.500
Yeah, it doesn't just fail, it actually accelerates the crash because it has zero capacity to understand a fundamentally broken present state.

06:11.560 --> 06:12.000
Exactly.

06:12.620 --> 06:16.180
It lacks what the affliction researchers call structural awareness.

06:16.580 --> 06:18.040
Structural awareness, okay.

06:18.080 --> 06:21.820
And that failure leads directly into the failure of optimization.

06:22.400 --> 06:25.240
Now, this is the one that really resonated with me.

06:25.240 --> 06:31.940
Because everything today, especially in tech and business, is just entirely obsessed with optimization.

06:31.940 --> 06:33.480
It's the buzzword of the decade.

06:33.580 --> 06:33.920
Totally.

06:34.360 --> 06:41.000
But optimization is like figuring out how to drive a car at the absolute maximum speed down a highway.

06:41.260 --> 06:41.560
Right.

06:41.760 --> 06:44.940
You tune the engine, you reduce drag, you perfect the steering.

06:45.460 --> 06:50.160
And that's great and all, but it doesn't tell you what to do when the bridge ahead suddenly collapses.

06:50.380 --> 06:50.940
Not at all.

06:50.940 --> 06:53.400
Optimization doesn't teach you how to build a raft.

06:53.560 --> 06:55.500
I think that captures the limitation perfectly.

06:55.720 --> 06:55.840
Yeah.

06:56.060 --> 07:06.140
A system that is maximizing an objective function, driving at top speed, as you said, is entirely blind to what the flipsion corpus terms inadmissible landscapes.

07:06.800 --> 07:07.700
Inadmissible landscapes.

07:07.920 --> 07:09.220
What does that actually mean?

07:09.580 --> 07:13.500
Well, an optimizing algorithm simply follows the steepest gradient toward a goal.

07:13.840 --> 07:15.580
It only knows how to climb the hill it's on.

07:15.580 --> 07:15.860
Okay.

07:16.180 --> 07:16.420
Yeah.

07:16.560 --> 07:22.340
It has no principled account of what to do when the landscape itself undergoes a catastrophic shift.

07:22.500 --> 07:22.700
Right.

07:22.920 --> 07:25.940
It's tuned for the highway, so when it hits water, it just sinks faster.

07:26.320 --> 07:26.800
Exactly.

07:27.240 --> 07:30.580
And this ties into why pure state descriptions fail, too.

07:30.920 --> 07:31.220
Okay.

07:31.400 --> 07:32.380
I'll pack that for me.

07:32.500 --> 07:37.740
The text argues that two identical states might have been reached via completely different histories.

07:38.160 --> 07:38.700
Like how?

07:38.940 --> 07:42.260
Let's say you have two identical servers running the exact same software.

07:42.380 --> 07:42.680
Okay.

07:42.880 --> 07:45.080
Server A was built yesterday, completely fresh.

07:45.080 --> 07:45.600
Got it.

07:46.300 --> 07:48.960
Server B, though, has been running for five years.

07:49.580 --> 07:55.840
It's been constantly patched, repeatedly compromised and clean, and it's deeply entangled with legacy databases.

07:56.060 --> 07:56.220
Okay.

07:56.340 --> 07:59.980
So a pure state description would say they are literally the same right now.

08:00.080 --> 08:00.440
Exactly.

08:01.040 --> 08:08.220
But their histories dictate entirely different meanings, totally different vulnerabilities, and different potential futures.

08:08.720 --> 08:12.100
So you really can't just take a snapshot and understand the system.

08:12.240 --> 08:12.620
You cannot.

08:12.620 --> 08:17.280
And finally, they realize that standard information theory doesn't work either.

08:17.380 --> 08:19.400
Because that's just counting bits, right?

08:19.440 --> 08:19.820
Pretty much.

08:19.940 --> 08:23.960
It essentially just measures the volume of data and the signal-to-noise ratio.

08:24.060 --> 08:24.260
Right.

08:24.260 --> 08:33.240
But counting bits doesn't explain why some boundaries and structures survive noise and chaos, while others completely collapse under the exact same conditions.

08:33.520 --> 08:34.420
That's a great point.

08:35.060 --> 08:39.460
I mean, think about our modern algorithmic feeds or corporate structures for a second.

08:39.620 --> 08:39.880
Okay.

08:39.880 --> 08:44.700
They are entirely focused on optimization, prediction, and information theory.

08:45.200 --> 08:46.100
They count the bits.

08:46.520 --> 08:48.280
They predict the user's next click.

08:48.460 --> 08:50.420
They optimize the revenue funnel.

08:50.540 --> 08:51.540
They do it very well, too.

08:51.580 --> 08:52.020
They do.

08:52.280 --> 08:57.320
But they lack repair mechanisms for when the underlying social fabric degrades.

08:57.320 --> 08:58.040
Exactly.

08:58.520 --> 09:08.240
When the cultural conditions that allowed a social media platform to thrive suddenly shift, all the optimization in the world won't save it from becoming a ghost town.

09:08.400 --> 09:10.580
Because optimization isn't survival.

09:10.820 --> 09:11.680
It really isn't.

09:11.980 --> 09:18.820
So because optimization and representation failed to explain survival, the researchers basically had to go back to the drawing board.

09:18.820 --> 09:20.240
They needed a whole new paradigm.

09:20.440 --> 09:20.640
Right.

09:20.940 --> 09:23.820
They had to invent a completely new foundational vocabulary.

09:24.420 --> 09:28.500
And this leads us to what the text calls the principal chain of dependencies.

09:28.860 --> 09:29.080
Okay.

09:29.180 --> 09:30.060
The principal chain.

09:30.340 --> 09:34.060
This is the core framework of the entire Flyxion universe.

09:34.880 --> 09:37.400
Distinction, memory, repair, and continuation.

09:37.840 --> 09:39.820
Distinction, memory, repair, and continuation.

09:40.220 --> 09:42.400
So this is basically the anatomy of survival.

09:42.520 --> 09:42.900
Exactly.

09:42.900 --> 09:53.420
It argues that for any structure to persist, whether it's a cell, a law, a software program, it has to successfully navigate this specific sequence.

09:53.540 --> 09:53.720
Okay.

09:53.760 --> 09:54.540
Let's go through them.

09:54.800 --> 10:00.520
It begins with the ontological critique program, which establishes the very first link, which is distinction.

10:01.240 --> 10:03.780
Now, this part of the text really threw me for a loop.

10:04.180 --> 10:07.740
It states that entities are not primitive, but distinctions are primitive.

10:08.260 --> 10:10.760
It's a tough concept to wrap your head around at first.

10:10.760 --> 10:13.860
Yeah, they're basically arguing that a thing isn't actually a thing.

10:13.980 --> 10:14.920
It's just a boundary.

10:15.260 --> 10:15.340
Right.

10:15.420 --> 10:21.120
It really forces you to totally rethink how reality is constructed.

10:21.260 --> 10:24.200
Instead of cataloging objects, you have to analyze differences.

10:24.640 --> 10:24.880
Okay.

10:24.940 --> 10:25.560
Give me an example.

10:25.760 --> 10:26.260
Look at a mountain.

10:26.740 --> 10:27.960
We think of a mountain as an object.

10:28.500 --> 10:31.440
But a mountain isn't a fundamental irreducible entity.

10:31.560 --> 10:32.220
It's not.

10:32.440 --> 10:32.660
No.

10:32.860 --> 10:37.900
It's merely a distinction in elevation that is temporarily being maintained against the forces of erosion.

10:38.280 --> 10:38.840
Oh, wow.

10:38.840 --> 10:44.880
The moment the erosion wins, the distinction vanishes, and the mountain just ceases to be.

10:45.100 --> 10:50.840
I think I'm struggling with that a bit when we move away from, like, physical geology and into abstract concepts.

10:51.080 --> 10:51.380
Sure.

10:51.480 --> 10:53.000
Let's look at abstract concepts.

10:53.180 --> 10:57.420
Well, the text says a word is just a distinction stabilized by social use.

10:57.480 --> 10:57.600
Yeah.

10:57.600 --> 11:01.980
But what happens when slang totally changes the meaning of a word over a decade?

11:02.440 --> 11:06.700
Does the original word die, or did the boundary just move?

11:06.800 --> 11:07.880
A boundary moved.

11:08.180 --> 11:10.720
Which means a new distinction was negotiated.

11:11.100 --> 11:11.480
Oh, okay.

11:11.560 --> 11:11.880
I see.

11:12.020 --> 11:17.220
A word is really just a boundary of sound and meaning pushed back against the noise of misunderstanding.

11:17.860 --> 11:19.260
That's a poetic way to put it.

11:19.260 --> 11:19.480
Yeah.

11:19.620 --> 11:26.640
And if society stops enforcing that specific boundary, the distinction collapses and the word just becomes archaic noise.

11:26.840 --> 11:27.120
Mm-hmm.

11:27.180 --> 11:30.640
So survival, therefore, has to start with establishing a distinction.

11:30.900 --> 11:31.120
Okay.

11:31.220 --> 11:38.980
So if a mountain or a word is just this fragile distinction fighting against erosion, the immediate problem is that distinctions fade, right?

11:39.100 --> 11:40.040
Inevitably, yes.

11:40.040 --> 11:43.720
So how does a system bring a fated distinction back?

11:43.780 --> 11:48.200
It has to have some way to, like, recall the shape of what was lost.

11:48.380 --> 11:48.680
Yes.

11:49.060 --> 11:51.780
Which brings us to the second link in the chain, memory.

11:52.580 --> 11:57.620
But the Flyxion definition of memory is not, you know, a hard drive.

11:57.740 --> 11:57.940
No.

11:58.060 --> 12:01.320
They totally reject the idea of memory as just a storage container.

12:01.580 --> 12:02.320
So what is it?

12:02.680 --> 12:06.200
They define memory as a recoverability structure.

12:06.920 --> 12:07.880
Recoverability structure.

12:07.880 --> 12:13.480
It is the geometric property of histories that allows past distinctions to be reconstructed.

12:13.540 --> 12:13.780
Okay.

12:14.020 --> 12:19.900
And they use a very specific, highly technical term for this reconstructive process, ecfury.

12:20.220 --> 12:20.620
Ecfury.

12:20.760 --> 12:21.400
I had to look that one up.

12:21.600 --> 12:23.620
It means reconstructing from partial cues.

12:23.820 --> 12:24.220
Exactly.

12:24.360 --> 12:27.120
So it's not pulling a fully formed file from a filing cabinet.

12:27.300 --> 12:28.020
No, not at all.

12:28.080 --> 12:31.200
It's more like finding the mold of a fossil in the mud.

12:31.300 --> 12:31.560
Right.

12:31.760 --> 12:35.220
And pouring plaster into it to figure out what the dinosaur looked like.

12:35.340 --> 12:36.400
That's a perfect analogy.

12:36.400 --> 12:38.560
You don't have the dinosaur itself anymore, obviously.

12:39.180 --> 12:46.020
But the empty space it left behind, the partial cues in the environment, lets you rebuild the shape of the distinction.

12:46.520 --> 12:49.400
That hits the core mechanism of ecfury perfectly.

12:50.120 --> 12:50.520
Awesome.

12:50.600 --> 12:56.080
Traditional retrieval implies the thing was sitting in a drawer, fully intact, just waiting to be pulled out.

12:56.180 --> 12:56.380
Right.

12:56.440 --> 12:57.720
Like a saved Word document.

12:58.060 --> 12:58.580
Exactly.

12:59.000 --> 13:02.140
But ecfury acknowledges that the thing was actually gone.

13:02.620 --> 13:03.260
It degraded.

13:03.280 --> 13:03.940
It vanished.

13:03.940 --> 13:09.620
But the shape of the space it left behind in the surrounding structure allowed the system to rebuild it on the fly.

13:09.980 --> 13:10.220
Fascinating.

13:10.380 --> 13:18.820
So if you have distinctions and you have this reconstructive ecphoric memory, you inevitably hit the third link, which is repair.

13:19.160 --> 13:19.400
Right.

13:19.400 --> 13:25.720
And the text makes a massive point of emphasizing that repair is constructive, not conservative.

13:26.100 --> 13:27.060
This is crucial.

13:27.320 --> 13:28.560
It creates new pathways.

13:28.820 --> 13:29.760
It doesn't just go backwards.

13:29.960 --> 13:30.120
Right.

13:30.320 --> 13:31.880
Because the landscape has changed.

13:32.000 --> 13:32.140
Right.

13:32.400 --> 13:36.180
When a distinction degrades, the environment around it has also shifted.

13:36.260 --> 13:37.760
You can't just return to equilibrium.

13:38.180 --> 13:39.100
You really can't.

13:39.460 --> 13:39.660
Yeah.

13:39.660 --> 13:48.080
If a bridge is damaged in a storm that also rewired of the river beneath it, repairing the bridge exactly as it was built originally would be catastrophic.

13:48.240 --> 13:49.260
Oh, because the river moved.

13:49.580 --> 13:49.960
Exactly.

13:50.740 --> 13:53.320
The repaired bridge must differ from the original.

13:54.100 --> 13:56.520
Repair inherently generates novelty.

13:57.200 --> 14:04.140
And that leads to the fourth link, continuation, where identity shifts from physical substance to trajectory.

14:04.480 --> 14:04.660
Right.

14:04.660 --> 14:06.780
But wait, I really have to push back here for a second.

14:06.920 --> 14:07.300
Go for it.

14:07.600 --> 14:18.200
If repair means the system is never actually identical to its previous state, and continuation isn't about physical substance at all, doesn't the original thing just die?

14:18.860 --> 14:19.240
How do you mean?

14:19.300 --> 14:19.940
Well, think about it.

14:19.940 --> 14:32.880
If I repair a wooden bridge over the course of, say, 50 years, and I end up replacing every single bolt, every beam, and every cable until there isn't a single original piece of wood left.

14:33.160 --> 14:33.420
Okay.

14:33.420 --> 14:34.840
Isn't that just a new bridge?

14:34.940 --> 14:35.880
How is that continuation?

14:36.240 --> 14:39.940
I mean, this is basically just the classic ship of Theseus paradox, isn't it?

14:40.160 --> 14:42.440
At what point does it stop being the same ship?

14:42.860 --> 14:44.640
It is exactly the ship of Theseus.

14:44.760 --> 14:44.980
Right.

14:45.100 --> 14:48.940
But the Flyxion framework actually solves it through the geometry of trajectories.

14:49.160 --> 14:49.960
Oh, okay.

14:50.440 --> 14:50.920
Let's hear it.

14:51.180 --> 14:55.620
The genius of the principle chain is that it proves persistence is not the conservation of matter.

14:55.720 --> 14:56.580
Okay, then what is it?

14:57.180 --> 14:59.680
Persistence is the conservation of continuation.

15:00.360 --> 15:01.920
Conservation of continuation?

15:01.920 --> 15:04.800
Yes, identity is the trajectory itself.

15:04.980 --> 15:06.040
Oh, wow.

15:06.140 --> 15:06.860
Think about a river.

15:07.340 --> 15:14.280
A river remains the exact same river, even though every single drop of water changes second by second.

15:14.340 --> 15:14.680
Right.

15:15.020 --> 15:16.340
Or think about the English language.

15:16.340 --> 15:23.580
It remains English, despite the vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation shifting entirely over a thousand years.

15:23.580 --> 15:26.820
So the parts change, but the trajectory doesn't.

15:26.880 --> 15:27.240
Exactly.

15:27.360 --> 15:31.540
As long as the trajectory of repair and ek-free remains unbroken, the entity survives.

15:31.960 --> 15:33.180
That is profound.

15:33.800 --> 15:37.620
So, in that sense, the ship of Theseus never actually sank.

15:37.880 --> 15:38.140
Never.

15:38.480 --> 15:40.700
Because the ship was never the wood in the first place.

15:41.120 --> 15:44.960
The ship was the continuous act of repairing a distinction against the ocean.

15:44.960 --> 15:45.540
Exactly.

15:45.720 --> 15:49.460
And the text outlines this explicitly in some of the major monographs mentioned in the catalog.

15:50.240 --> 15:53.480
Specifically, Persistence Before Truth in Holonomic Space.

15:53.600 --> 15:54.440
I saw those titles.

15:54.700 --> 15:55.520
They sound intense.

15:55.720 --> 15:57.900
They make a very radical philosophical claim.

15:58.320 --> 16:01.300
They argue that persistence is a more primitive category than truth.

16:01.520 --> 16:02.840
That is a dense claim.

16:03.240 --> 16:04.580
Persistence Before Truth.

16:04.900 --> 16:06.300
What does that actually mean in practice?

16:06.300 --> 16:18.260
It means that before you can even evaluate whether a scientific theory, a cultural narrative, or, you know, a line of code is true or false, it first has to survive long enough to be evaluated.

16:18.480 --> 16:19.440
Oh, wow.

16:19.540 --> 16:21.480
Truth is a luxury of stable systems.

16:21.660 --> 16:22.780
That is such a good quote.

16:22.900 --> 16:24.860
Truth is a luxury of stable systems.

16:25.060 --> 16:25.600
It really is.

16:25.680 --> 16:25.820
Yeah.

16:25.920 --> 16:31.900
If a system cannot maintain its distinction through repair, questions of truth are completely irrelevant.

16:32.380 --> 16:34.220
Survival precedes accuracy.

16:34.220 --> 16:36.440
Okay, so we have this powerful chain.

16:36.640 --> 16:38.940
Distinction, memory, repair, continuation.

16:39.440 --> 16:39.640
Right.

16:39.980 --> 16:43.340
A system manages to repair its boundaries and continue its trajectory.

16:43.820 --> 16:48.280
But once a system achieves continuation, we have to ask a frankly terrifying question.

16:48.560 --> 16:48.940
Which is?

16:49.040 --> 16:50.540
Where is it continuing to?

16:50.720 --> 16:51.780
Ah, yes.

16:52.080 --> 16:56.560
And this brings us to the climax concept of the entire 100-page metadocument.

16:56.720 --> 16:57.460
This is the big one.

16:57.460 --> 17:03.160
This is the unifying theory discovered across all 15 distinct research programs in the Flyxion Corpus.

17:03.160 --> 17:04.440
This is admissibility.

17:04.700 --> 17:05.300
Admissibility.

17:05.780 --> 17:11.260
The text defines it as the property of being compatible with the continuation of a system.

17:11.580 --> 17:15.660
In spatial terms, it is the geometry of the futures that remain reachable.

17:15.760 --> 17:16.940
The geometry of futures.

17:17.040 --> 17:17.220
Okay.

17:17.420 --> 17:21.320
The text talks about admissibility manifolds and reachability volumes.

17:22.580 --> 17:27.940
Imagine possibility as an actual physical landscape stretching out in front of a system.

17:28.080 --> 17:29.600
Like a terrain you have to navigate.

17:29.600 --> 17:30.040
Exactly.

17:30.440 --> 17:36.640
So every decision a system makes, every piece of damage it takes, it physically changes the shape of that landscape.

17:36.900 --> 17:37.160
Yes.

17:37.260 --> 17:43.080
And the futures you can still physically or logically access, that makes up your reachability volume.

17:43.220 --> 17:43.740
Exactly.

17:44.420 --> 17:47.660
And admissibility sits entirely prior to truth.

17:47.820 --> 17:48.800
Prior to truth again.

17:48.980 --> 17:49.240
Right.

17:49.400 --> 17:51.940
A proposition or an action isn't necessarily false.

17:52.040 --> 17:53.420
It might just be inadmissible.

17:53.660 --> 17:55.280
Because doing it would ruin everything.

17:55.280 --> 17:55.720
Yes.

17:55.940 --> 18:00.880
If entertaining it would destroy the distinction structure of the system itself, it is inadmissible.

18:01.240 --> 18:04.160
You know, here's an analogy that really helped me grasp this concept.

18:04.280 --> 18:04.600
Play the theory.

18:05.040 --> 18:06.160
Think of a game of chess.

18:06.620 --> 18:09.820
Admissibility isn't about how many pieces you have on the board right now.

18:09.980 --> 18:10.200
Right.

18:10.260 --> 18:11.340
Because that's optimization.

18:11.620 --> 18:12.040
Exactly.

18:12.600 --> 18:14.100
Here's where it gets really interesting.

18:14.860 --> 18:19.700
Admissibility is about how many valid legal moves you have left before checkmate.

18:19.920 --> 18:23.760
That perfectly captures the difference between optimization and admissibility.

18:23.760 --> 18:24.160
Right.

18:24.440 --> 18:27.320
Because you can have a board full of high-value pieces.

18:27.680 --> 18:29.380
Queens, rooks, knights, whatever.

18:29.580 --> 18:31.580
But if your king is cornered...

18:31.580 --> 18:31.840
Yeah.

18:32.060 --> 18:36.540
If your king is cornered and you only have one legal move left and that move ultimately

18:36.540 --> 18:42.940
forces a mate, well, your reachability volume is virtually zero, even if you're rich in pieces.

18:43.200 --> 18:48.160
And the text points out that admissibility is emphatically not the same as stability.

18:48.320 --> 18:49.580
This was a huge point.

18:49.680 --> 18:53.260
They use the ancient Maya civilization as a historical example of this trap.

18:53.260 --> 18:56.960
It's a really sobering analysis of civilizational collapse.

18:57.140 --> 18:57.380
It is.

18:57.660 --> 19:02.480
The text notes that a society can be locally optimal-like, incredibly efficient at extracting

19:02.480 --> 19:08.600
resources, perfectly stable in its hierarchy right up until it completely exhausts its admissibility

19:08.600 --> 19:09.580
and collapses.

19:09.800 --> 19:10.100
Well, wait.

19:10.200 --> 19:12.100
Why does it collapse if it's currently stable?

19:12.360 --> 19:14.880
Because its reachability volume shrinks to zero.

19:15.340 --> 19:16.320
It runs out of moves.

19:16.320 --> 19:22.760
The Maya collapse, at least as modeled in this specific framework, wasn't because they were

19:22.760 --> 19:24.680
inherently unstable in the short term.

19:25.020 --> 19:30.560
The collapse occurred because they consumed all of their admissible futures just to maintain

19:30.560 --> 19:31.940
that short-term stability.

19:32.200 --> 19:35.200
So they basically burned their own bridges to stay warm today.

19:35.320 --> 19:35.640
Exactly.

19:35.860 --> 19:41.480
So when a massive drought hit, they had no alternative agricultural trajectories left.

19:41.680 --> 19:43.900
They had optimized themselves right into a corner.

19:43.900 --> 19:47.560
So a collapsed society might look incredibly rich on paper.

19:47.860 --> 19:51.700
It might boast massive monuments and totally stable supply chains.

19:52.360 --> 19:55.380
But structurally, it has no moves left.

19:55.660 --> 19:55.900
Precise.

19:56.060 --> 20:01.180
And what's wild to me is that the Flyxion corpus doesn't just apply this geometry of

20:01.180 --> 20:04.640
survival to, you know, societies or chess games.

20:04.680 --> 20:06.120
No, they take it much, much further.

20:06.280 --> 20:10.040
They literally map this onto the underlying fabric of the universe itself.

20:10.160 --> 20:10.440
Yes.

20:10.680 --> 20:12.620
Through what they call the RSVP program.

20:12.620 --> 20:17.160
RSVP, which stands for the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum.

20:17.760 --> 20:20.900
It sounds like something you'd need a PhD in theoretical physics just to pronounce.

20:21.100 --> 20:21.660
Oh, absolutely.

20:21.840 --> 20:24.560
But it is their primary cosmological physics program, right?

20:24.800 --> 20:25.280
It is.

20:25.620 --> 20:30.320
They argue that this survival problem isn't just a cognitive or social phenomenon.

20:30.560 --> 20:30.800
Right.

20:31.260 --> 20:36.780
Using a mathematical framework they developed, which they call CTOV equations, they argue

20:36.780 --> 20:42.060
that cosmic structure itself is a distinction maintained against entropic erosion.

20:42.060 --> 20:48.980
So like gravity, entropy, the formation of entire galaxies, it's all just the universe trying

20:48.980 --> 20:50.480
to maintain its admissibility.

20:50.620 --> 20:51.300
That's the argument.

20:51.640 --> 20:56.520
Let's unpack these CTOV equations really quickly because the text relies on them pretty heavily.

20:56.820 --> 20:57.240
We should.

20:57.460 --> 21:01.620
It stands for capacity, transport, orientation, and volume.

21:01.620 --> 21:05.180
What are these variables actually measuring in the real world?

21:05.440 --> 21:06.500
Let's break them down one by one.

21:06.740 --> 21:10.260
Capacity is just the sheer amount of systemic energy or resources available.

21:10.480 --> 21:10.680
Okay.

21:10.760 --> 21:12.100
So for a star, that's hydrogen.

21:12.440 --> 21:12.760
Exactly.

21:12.960 --> 21:16.000
For a society, it might be a grain surplus or economic capital.

21:16.120 --> 21:16.620
Makes sense.

21:16.980 --> 21:17.860
Then you have transport.

21:18.420 --> 21:21.600
Transport is the friction of moving that capacity across boundaries.

21:21.600 --> 21:24.020
Oh, like how hard it is to use the resources.

21:24.220 --> 21:24.580
Exactly.

21:24.960 --> 21:27.560
How easily can the star fuse that hydrogen?

21:27.900 --> 21:30.240
How easily can the society distribute that grain?

21:30.580 --> 21:30.820
Okay.

21:30.960 --> 21:35.680
So capacity is what you have and transport is how easily you can move it.

21:35.780 --> 21:36.180
Precisely.

21:36.600 --> 21:37.660
Next is orientation.

21:38.380 --> 21:40.580
This is the vector or direction of that movement.

21:40.580 --> 21:40.760
Right.

21:40.840 --> 21:41.180
So going.

21:41.440 --> 21:41.640
Right.

21:41.960 --> 21:46.740
Is the capacity being applied toward maintaining the distinction like repairing the bridge?

21:46.740 --> 21:51.500
Or is it being misdirected in a way that erodes the boundary?

21:51.700 --> 21:52.160
Got it.

21:52.260 --> 21:52.760
And the V.

21:53.160 --> 21:54.400
Finally, volume.

21:54.920 --> 21:57.940
This is the literal size of the admissibility manifold.

21:58.820 --> 22:03.820
How many viable futures are kept open by this specific arrangement of capacity and transport?

22:04.100 --> 22:04.440
Wow.

22:04.440 --> 22:09.200
So the universe itself is a system actively solving the survival problem.

22:09.740 --> 22:12.060
The cosmic microwave background, the orbit of planets.

22:12.180 --> 22:15.980
It's just the universe maintaining physical distinctions against the pull of entropy.

22:15.980 --> 22:20.540
Constantly navigating its own reachability volume using those four variables.

22:20.780 --> 22:23.340
It scales all the way from the subatomic to the galactic.

22:23.500 --> 22:24.640
That is mind-blowing.

22:24.880 --> 22:28.200
But of course, theories and equations are just abstractions at the end of the day.

22:28.500 --> 22:32.660
A theory makes claims, but an instrument makes phenomena observable.

22:33.380 --> 22:37.640
If admissibility and distinction are real, physical, or geometric properties,

22:38.080 --> 22:40.300
the researchers had to build tools to look at them.

22:40.420 --> 22:44.340
Which brings us perfectly to section four of the catalog, the instruments.

22:44.340 --> 22:47.220
Making the invisible inspectable.

22:47.400 --> 22:48.720
This section is fascinating.

22:48.800 --> 22:55.320
They cataloged an unbelievable array of physical, computational, and visualization instruments.

22:55.500 --> 22:56.100
Where do you want to start?

22:56.460 --> 22:59.620
Let's start with the visual ones, like the Lava Thought Visualizer.

22:59.800 --> 23:01.640
Ah, the Lava Thought Visualizer.

23:02.100 --> 23:07.200
It was designed to map semantic terrain as a dynamic topographic landscape.

23:07.200 --> 23:12.520
Okay, so it takes the abstract concept of meaning and literally gives it spatial geometry.

23:12.940 --> 23:13.180
Exactly.

23:13.260 --> 23:14.720
How does that actually work?

23:14.780 --> 23:18.520
Like if I plug a concept into this visualizer, what am I literally seeing?

23:18.700 --> 23:22.140
You're seeing conceptual proximity rendered physically.

23:22.380 --> 23:22.660
Okay.

23:22.660 --> 23:27.460
So if two ideas are closely related, they appear as hills that are close together on the landscape.

23:27.520 --> 23:27.960
Oh, I see.

23:28.240 --> 23:34.100
And as cultural or linguistic usage shifts over time, and those concepts drift apart in meaning,

23:34.940 --> 23:39.020
you literally watch the topographic landscape deform in real time.

23:39.140 --> 23:39.880
No way!

23:40.040 --> 23:40.280
Yes.

23:40.780 --> 23:45.140
A valley might open up between them, representing a loss of ephoric connection.

23:45.300 --> 23:46.660
That is so cool.

23:46.760 --> 23:48.680
And they also built the audioscope, right?

23:48.840 --> 23:49.320
They did.

23:49.320 --> 23:56.140
Which uses, like, old CRT phosphor aesthetics to render audio, but it doesn't just show a

23:56.140 --> 23:58.400
standard amplitude wave, like a normal EQ.

23:58.800 --> 23:59.080
No.

23:59.340 --> 24:01.300
It renders sound as a spatial trajectory.

24:01.840 --> 24:05.600
It shows the continuation of the audio signal's identity through noise.

24:05.680 --> 24:08.060
And most importantly, they developed reachability landscapes.

24:08.480 --> 24:09.060
These are key.

24:09.440 --> 24:14.340
They visualize those admissibility structures we discussed earlier as navigable 3D terrain.

24:14.340 --> 24:19.200
So high ground represents, like, an accessible, safe future trajectory.

24:19.600 --> 24:19.740
Yes.

24:19.840 --> 24:24.620
And a sheer cliff represents a catastrophic boundary where the distinction collapses.

24:24.900 --> 24:25.220
Exactly.

24:25.420 --> 24:29.380
And a valley floor might be an attractor estate the system just naturally falls into.

24:29.480 --> 24:33.140
So you can literally walk around in the possibility space of a system.

24:33.320 --> 24:33.820
It's incredible.

24:34.060 --> 24:36.440
But, you know, visual tools only show you the output.

24:36.840 --> 24:42.540
They needed computational instruments to rethink how machines operate on these principles from

24:42.540 --> 24:43.280
the inside out.

24:43.280 --> 24:44.680
Which leads to SpherePop.

24:45.460 --> 24:45.800
Yes.

24:46.060 --> 24:51.100
They developed an entirely new programming paradigm and a language called SpherePop.

24:51.300 --> 24:55.060
The text mentions it's based on event histories rather than static states.

24:55.320 --> 24:55.600
Right.

24:55.920 --> 24:59.840
A traditional computer program just looks at the data sitting in its memory right now.

24:59.920 --> 25:01.040
It just sees what's there.

25:01.380 --> 25:06.180
But SpherePop looks at the entire history of how that data was constructed over time.

25:06.180 --> 25:11.240
But the computational instrument that truly stuck me in my tracks was the refusal calculus.

25:11.480 --> 25:13.260
Oh, the refusal calculus is brilliant.

25:13.400 --> 25:17.400
What does it actually mean for a computer program to have a refusal calculus?

25:17.620 --> 25:19.220
Like a machine just saying no.

25:19.500 --> 25:23.100
This raises a really important question about how we currently view computation.

25:23.320 --> 25:23.600
Okay.

25:23.880 --> 25:28.560
In our standard paradigm, computation is purely about executing commands to arrive at a state,

25:28.680 --> 25:28.900
right?

25:28.900 --> 25:29.380
Yeah.

25:29.540 --> 25:31.340
You tell it to do something, it does it.

25:31.520 --> 25:34.400
And if a program encounters a command, it cannot execute.

25:34.700 --> 25:35.280
It crashes.

25:35.700 --> 25:36.460
It throws an error.

25:36.620 --> 25:37.880
A blue screen of death.

25:38.180 --> 25:38.580
Exactly.

25:38.920 --> 25:40.540
And we view that as a failure of the machine.

25:40.920 --> 25:45.860
But in the Flyxion framework, refusal is treated as a first-class mathematical operation.

25:46.580 --> 25:48.560
That completely flips our standard view.

25:48.760 --> 25:50.180
So an error isn't a failure.

25:50.420 --> 25:51.260
No, not at all.

25:51.640 --> 25:56.700
A computation declining to proceed is an event with profound semantic content.

25:57.000 --> 25:57.260
Okay.

25:57.400 --> 25:57.900
Explain that.

25:57.900 --> 26:04.080
When a machine utilizing the refusal calculus stops, it is actively mapping the edge of the

26:04.080 --> 26:05.360
admissibility manifold.

26:05.660 --> 26:06.160
Oh, wow.

26:06.160 --> 26:10.920
It is essentially saying, if I execute this command, the distinction structure of the

26:10.920 --> 26:11.760
system will collapse.

26:12.060 --> 26:17.320
Therefore, I formally refuse to take the step in order to preserve the continuation of the

26:17.320 --> 26:17.580
system.

26:17.920 --> 26:20.600
The refusal is the machine protecting itself.

26:20.900 --> 26:21.300
Exactly.

26:21.420 --> 26:25.900
It's finding the cliff edge on that reachability landscape we just talked about and actively

26:25.900 --> 26:27.320
choosing not to jump off.

26:27.320 --> 26:30.940
It makes refusal an act of survival rather than an act of failure.

26:31.140 --> 26:31.940
That is amazing.

26:32.320 --> 26:34.620
And the researchers didn't stop at software either.

26:34.620 --> 26:38.080
They applied these instruments to human knowledge and media.

26:38.520 --> 26:38.600
Right.

26:38.600 --> 26:42.420
They created publishing instruments under their document ecology program.

26:42.420 --> 26:45.420
Things like Marquant's and readability first publishing.

26:45.420 --> 26:50.560
Because documents are structures that have to survive across vast spans of time.

26:50.560 --> 26:51.800
Yeah, centuries sometimes.

26:51.800 --> 26:56.560
A Marquant is described as a self-describing structural tag.

26:57.300 --> 26:59.740
It's metadata designed to degrade gracefully.

26:59.980 --> 27:01.160
Degrade gracefully.

27:01.260 --> 27:02.100
What does that mean?

27:02.640 --> 27:06.560
Well, if a future computer doesn't know how to read the complex formatting of a file,

27:07.400 --> 27:10.880
the Marquant at least preserves the foundational distinctions of the text.

27:10.880 --> 27:13.400
So it might not look pretty, but you can still read it.

27:13.460 --> 27:13.800
Exactly.

27:14.120 --> 27:16.480
Which ties right into readability first publishing.

27:16.620 --> 27:21.640
This is the philosophy of explicitly sacrificing presentational richness, right?

27:21.840 --> 27:22.120
Yes.

27:22.480 --> 27:27.740
So getting rid of fancy layouts, complex embedded video players, proprietary fonts,

27:28.100 --> 27:30.980
all for the sake of long-term document survival.

27:31.120 --> 27:35.260
Because typographic simplicity is actually a calculated survival strategy.

27:35.260 --> 27:36.360
That makes total sense.

27:36.480 --> 27:40.240
I mean, a complex PDF might become entirely unreadable in 20 years

27:40.240 --> 27:42.900
when the proprietary software rendering it goes obsolete.

27:43.200 --> 27:47.960
But a simple text file with clear, unformatted distinctions will survive for centuries.

27:48.320 --> 27:49.840
It really forces us to ask,

27:50.520 --> 27:53.920
are we optimizing our documents to look beautiful today,

27:54.020 --> 27:56.620
or are we optimizing them to be admissible tomorrow?

27:57.000 --> 27:57.860
It's a great question.

27:58.140 --> 28:00.680
But, you know, the text acknowledges a limitation here.

28:00.860 --> 28:01.300
What's that?

28:01.640 --> 28:03.980
Mathematics, visualizers, coding languages.

28:03.980 --> 28:07.680
They can show you the shape of a collapse from the outside.

28:08.060 --> 28:08.180
Right.

28:08.420 --> 28:12.040
But they can't tell you what a collapse actually feels like from the inside.

28:12.460 --> 28:14.340
They lack subjective experience.

28:14.460 --> 28:15.720
They lack phenomenology, right.

28:15.820 --> 28:16.080
Yes.

28:16.660 --> 28:21.500
A topographic map of a valley doesn't tell you what it feels like to be starving in that valley.

28:21.660 --> 28:23.020
Oh, that's a chilling way to put it.

28:23.240 --> 28:26.900
And this is exactly why the Flyxion Project employs experimental fiction.

28:27.140 --> 28:28.660
It's not just a creative hobby for them.

28:28.720 --> 28:31.420
No, it's an actual rigorous laboratory.

28:31.420 --> 28:34.880
Section 5 details the fictional habitat program.

28:35.440 --> 28:40.980
They construct narrative environments where the reader becomes a participant observer in structural degradation.

28:41.320 --> 28:44.180
Like the screenplay included in the corpus, City Brutes.

28:44.520 --> 28:45.820
Yeah, tell me about that one.

28:46.240 --> 28:49.880
It describes an urban environment torn between two massive bureaucracies,

28:50.340 --> 28:53.760
the Library of Revisions and the Library of Contradictions.

28:54.100 --> 28:55.660
Sounds very Kafka-esque.

28:55.660 --> 28:56.340
Very.

28:56.940 --> 29:00.020
The city is an allegory for modern information environments,

29:00.220 --> 29:05.440
where maintaining meaning requires constant active effort against ambient entropy.

29:05.860 --> 29:10.840
But the text that truly exemplifies this whole idea is Notes from the Collapse Well.

29:10.980 --> 29:11.500
Oh, absolutely.

29:11.520 --> 29:18.320
It's an ergodic novel, which basically means it requires non-trivial active effort just to read the thing.

29:18.440 --> 29:18.660
Right.

29:18.660 --> 29:24.520
As you progress through the pages, the spelling, the grammar, and the physical typography intentionally degrade.

29:24.900 --> 29:27.360
It is an act of epistemological genius, frankly.

29:27.520 --> 29:28.140
It's wild.

29:28.220 --> 29:30.120
This isn't just an illustration of a theory.

29:30.660 --> 29:32.800
It is phenomenological evidence.

29:32.800 --> 29:37.580
Just imagine trying to read a book while the letters are actively forgetting how to be lettered.

29:37.600 --> 29:39.300
It sounds incredibly frustrating.

29:39.480 --> 29:40.000
It does.

29:40.000 --> 29:44.240
You, the reader, are forced to become the repair mechanism.

29:44.960 --> 29:51.020
You have to use ecfory reconstructing from partial cues to guess what the degraded words originally meant.

29:51.160 --> 29:51.920
And if you can't?

29:52.060 --> 29:54.640
Either you bridge the gap and rebuild the lost distinctions,

29:54.900 --> 29:58.340
or the meaning of the book literally dies right there in your hands.

29:58.580 --> 30:05.240
See, your failure or success in reading that corrupted text is the exact data the researchers are looking for.

30:05.240 --> 30:06.140
That's the experiment.

30:06.140 --> 30:06.500
Yes.

30:07.000 --> 30:12.260
A CTOV equation can mathematically model the rate at which a distinction boundary erodes,

30:12.460 --> 30:18.540
but only reading notes from the collapse well can tell you what the cognitive burden of that erosion actually feels like.

30:18.640 --> 30:24.760
Man, they also developed the Flyxion Instrumentarium, which is written like a medieval bestiary.

30:24.960 --> 30:25.180
Right.

30:25.260 --> 30:27.000
It catalogs conceptual entities?

30:27.300 --> 30:27.520
Yeah.

30:27.700 --> 30:35.460
Things like witnesses and repairers, and it assigns them ecological niches, diets, and threat statuses.

30:35.460 --> 30:36.740
Right, they're endangered animals.

30:37.000 --> 30:37.500
Exactly.

30:37.860 --> 30:44.580
And then there's Worlds of VIF, which is a series of speculative documentary texts that treat alternative histories not as fiction,

30:45.060 --> 30:48.200
but as archaeological evidence of abandoned trajectories.

30:48.320 --> 30:50.900
Reachable futures that were ultimately not selected.

30:51.100 --> 30:58.900
They just treat fiction as a bounded, controlled environment where abstract concepts, admissibility, repair, memory must compete for survival.

30:58.900 --> 31:01.000
It's a staggering approach to research, honestly.

31:01.320 --> 31:02.120
It really is.

31:02.200 --> 31:08.980
So we've looked at the cosmos, we've looked at computational refusal, and we've explored these ergodic fictional worlds.

31:09.120 --> 31:09.400
Right.

31:09.760 --> 31:15.560
But how does this framework diagnose the structures in our everyday real world reality?

31:15.560 --> 31:21.280
How does the Flyxion Catalog apply to the world we are literally living in right now?

31:21.380 --> 31:30.960
Well, the corpus actually devotes significant space to applying these theories to modern political economy, institutions, and what they call internet archaeology.

31:31.140 --> 31:37.140
Let's start with internet archaeology, because they analyzed massive data sets of GitHub developer survival curves.

31:37.200 --> 31:37.540
They did.

31:37.540 --> 31:43.600
